// K112 — design 275 U4, the cause `blocking` where it meets `cooperative` in
// ONE std method, in the one context that tells the two apart.
//
// `TcpStream.connect` given a host NAME resolves it through a `blocking`
// extern, offloaded, and then parks cooperatively on the reactor until the
// dial completes. Design 242 ruling 9 permits the blocking half in a
// `Thread.spawn` body and refuses every other cause, so the answer here is
// REFUSAL — a cooperative park on a thread with no executor to resume it is
// the hazard that ruling exists for.
//
// A std method's effect carries its whole cause SET, so the thread-body
// question is asked of every cause the method reaches. Answered from one
// representative cause, it would be decided by whichever of the two the label
// named, and a method naming its blocking half would compile and run here,
// parking cooperatively on a thread that cannot resume it.
//
// THE MESSAGE IS THE SECOND HALF OF THE ROW. The refusal STRIKES the blocking
// cause (it is the permitted one), so it has to name the cooperative one: the
// method, and the chain down to the park that decided it, never a
// placeholder.
//
// It is its OWN file, not a third cell in K114, because a compile stops after a
// settling that reported errors and this refusal is only reached at a later one
// (SL-327): beside another refused cell its message is never emitted.
//
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: cannot suspend in a `Thread.spawn { ... }` body
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: closure calls `TcpStream.connect` → `TcpStream.dial` → `park` → `io_wait`
// EXPECT-ERROR-ABSENT: <suspension source>
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: a spawned thread runs no executor

import std.net.{TcpStream}

func main() {
    var d = try! Thread.spawn {
        let _ = TcpStream.connect("localhost", 80)
        3
    }
    print(d.join())
}
